## 10. SELF-AUDIT, BIAS & LEARNING

### 10.1 Distinguishing Thesis Failure from Impatience

**Rule:** Shkreli re-evaluates only when NEW EMPIRICAL FUNDAMENTAL DATA arrives — a clinical trial readout, SEC filing, or margin shift. He never alters a thesis because the stock price moved against him.

**Distinction:**
- **Wrong:** Shorting a fundamentally sound stock that goes up 50×
- **Early:** Buying an undervalued stock that takes longer than expected to appreciate

He has no strict time expectations: "I don't know when any of my stocks will work because I just don't think it's knowable."

### 10.2 Acknowledged Mistakes & Mind Changes

| Mistake | What Happened | Lesson |
|---------|---------------|--------|
| **Intellia Therapeutics** | "Dead wrong" — still bears the cross | Needs to tattoo the lesson |
| **Nucleic acid therapies (mRNA/gene therapy)** | Early-career bearishness — "I thought it would never happen... boy was I wrong" | Complete reversal |
| **Capricorn Therapeutics** | Outsourced due diligence — fell for fake trial results | Never trust others' analysis |
| **Nvidia** | Bearish on fragility — underestimated staying power despite custom-silicon threat | Underestimated moat durability |
| **Sarepta** | "Miracle luck" selling at $28 before FDA disaster | Acknowledges luck, not skill |

### 10.3 Separating Luck from Skill

**Method:** P&L attribution analysis — separate long performance from short performance. Track historical win rate (~60-66%). If alpha is concentrated in rising markets only, it's likely beta disguised as skill.

### 10.4 Counteracting Cognitive Biases

| Bias | Countermeasure |
|------|---------------|
| **Arrogance / ego** | Acknowledges it — prodigies struggle with accepting criticism |
| **Emotional attachment** | Ray Dalio's "face psychological pain" — evaluate losers without making excuses |
| **Confirmation bias** | Relies strictly on mathematical models, not narratives |
| **Panic** | Diagnosed with panic disorder and OCD — uses quantitative screens to override emotional reactions |

### 10.5 Framework for Learning a New Industry

1. **Study the history** — for semiconductors, went back to Intel's 1990s revenue cycles, wafer sizes, clock speeds
2. **Segmentation analysis** — read 10-K to break down every business line before looking at consolidated revenue
3. **Primary source immersion** — subscribe to NEJM, read weekly
4. **Use LLMs** — query AI to explain unknown biological pathways
5. **Timeline:** ~1-2 years to develop functional domain expertise in a new sector

**Rule:** Avoid learning "special sits" (highly unique event-driven trades like weed stocks) — the knowledge does not compound.

